Context: in the third version of 'Analysis situs' (June 1986), Grothendieck is building a point-free, combinatorial axiomatics for low-dimensional 'tame' spaces. Here these are 'ficelles', 1-dimensional strings. An abstract structure M is a set of 'pieces' carrying relations: X ≪ Y (part of), X ∥ Y (disjoint), X ⊥ Y, X ≼ Y, and comb(X) (a decomposition of X). A distinguished subset L ⊂ M plays the role of elementary pieces, and the axioms Ax L 2 and Ax L 3 belong to L.

On p.73 he defines a 'realisation' of M on a point set P. This is a support map M → 𝔓(P), X ↦ |X|, subject to axioms (i)–(vi):
- monotone for ≪ and ⊂;
- intersections of overlapping pieces are unions of supports of common subpieces;
- disjointness ∥ corresponds exactly to disjoint supports;
- ≼ preserves supports;
- |X| is the union of the supports of its components;
- a T0 point-separation condition.
NB 2 on p.73 says |X| is determined by its values on L via (v). The axioms then reduce, modulo Ax L 2 and L 3, to a smaller set for the restricted map L → 𝔓(P).

This page states that reduced variant. The conditions are:
- (i) for x, y ∈ L, x ∥ y ⇔ |x| ∩ |y| = ∅;
- (ii) for ξ ≠ η in P there is x ∈ L with ξ ∈ |x| and η ∉ |x|, or the reverse. This is T0 separation of points by elementary supports.

Example: P is a topological ficelle (linear, ...) and M is the structure of its segments, i.e. sub-strings, with the obvious support. The same holds for 'linear' structures. He remarks that (ii) need not hold, for instance depending on whether the ends of the string are joined. A margin note links the univocity of (ii) to the strings being connected.

He then observes that in all interesting 1-dimensional cases (except perhaps structures attached to an ad hoc ordered set, transversal or not) there is a natural notion of supports. Hence the key question: does M alone determine P up to unique isomorphism? Concretely, is there a canonical (anti-)isomorphism between an ordered set built from M and 𝔓(P)? He names that ordered set ΣM, 'the set of abstract supports of M'.

The next page makes this precise. For A ⊂ M put cosupp A = {X : X ∥ A} and supp A = cosupp cosupp A. This is the biorthogonal (polar) closure for the disjointness relation, and ΣM is the complete lattice of closed sets. He tries to prove supp A ⊂ supp B ⇔ |A| ⊂ |B|, gets one direction, and finds the converse unclear in general.

In modern terms, the problem is to recover the points of a space from a mereotopological / orthogonality structure. The hoped-for statement is that the lattice of biorthogonally closed sets is the full power set of P: atomic Boolean, with atoms equal to points. That is plausible for finite combinatorial P (cells of a finite string). It fails for genuinely topological continua.
